Celtel: A Better Way
As mobile telephony grew in prominance during the last several decades,
the giant telecommunications companies invested in infrastructure world-
wide. In this spurt of development, Africa was largely ignored due to its
widespread instability, corruption, and poverty. Companies that attempted
to do business in Africa faced the unpleasant reality that bribes were often a
prerequisite to conducting business. In 1998, Mo Ibrahim, a consultant with
experience in African telecom companies, saw this as an opportunity.
     Mo founded Celtel, which is a mobile phone service provider, and took
an especially effective approach to doing business. Rather than targeting
individual African markets, he eliminated inefficiencies by widening the
scale of operations to a pancontinental level. This large scale also increased
the respectability and political clout of Celtel, allowing him to operate with-
out paying bribes. In this way, Mo was able to utilize the positive forces of
influence to achieve his goals rather than resorting to immoral pecuniary
incentives.
